---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/,'');
---
<BaseLayout title="Playground" description="Small experiments and side ideas by Muhammad Rayandika, AI Agent Engineer." active="playground">
  <header class="page-heading enter"><span class="hand">playground</span><h1 class="display">Just for fun</h1><p>A little collection of code, color, and things that spark ideas.</p></header>
  <div class="play-toolbar section-pad" hidden><div class="play-filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter playground"><button class="active" data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All the things</button><button data-filter="code" aria-pressed="false">Code & systems</button><button data-filter="visual" aria-pressed="false">Visual inspiration</button></div><button id="shuffle-desk" class="ink-link">Shuffle the desk ↗</button><span id="play-status" class="sr-only" role="status"></span></div>
  <section class="playground-board section-pad" aria-label="Creative notebook"><details class="play-card card-computer" data-category="code"><summary><span class="torn lavender hand play-label">hello world!</span><div class="retro-art" aria-hidden="true"><div class="retro-monitor"><div class="retro-screen"><span>hello<span class="cursor">_</span></span></div><div class="retro-bottom"><b>✦</b><span></span></div></div><div class="retro-foot"></div><div class="retro-keyboard"></div></div><span class="play-caption">The beginning of every good rabbit hole <span>+</span></span></summary><p>There is something satisfying about a tiny program doing exactly what you asked. A little CSS illustration, just for the joy of making it.</p></details><details class="play-card card-studio" data-category="visual"><summary><span class="torn butter hand play-label">room to think</span><img src={`${base}/images/scrapbook/studio.jpg`} alt="Sunlit studio with plants and desks" width="600" height="700" loading="lazy"/><span class="play-caption">Spaces that make room for ideas <span>+</span></span></summary><p>A bit of workspace inspiration. Natural light, a clear desk, and something green. Photo from Unsplash.</p></details><details class="play-card card-type" data-category="visual"><summary><span class="torn mint hand play-label">make it useful</span><div class="type-art" aria-hidden="true"><span>LESS<br/>BUT<br/><i>BETTER.</i></span><b>✳</b><small>A NOTE TO MYSELF</small></div><span class="play-caption">A small reminder, in big letters <span>+</span></span></summary><p>A type experiment and a principle I come back to: the useful version of an idea is often the simplest one.</p></details><details class="play-card card-circuit" data-category="visual"><summary><span class="torn blush hand play-label">under the hood</span><img src={`${base}/images/scrapbook/circuit.jpg`} alt="Detailed view of an electronic circuit board" width="600" height="700" loading="lazy"/><span class="play-caption">Small pieces. Many possibilities. <span>+</span></span></summary><p>A visual reminder that complex systems are made of smaller connections. Circuit photography from Unsplash.</p></details><details class="play-card card-flow" data-category="code"><summary><span class="torn butter hand play-label">connect the dots</span><div class="flow-art" aria-hidden="true"><span class="flow-bubble">a little idea ✦</span><i>↓</i><div><span>context</span><span>tools</span></div><i>↓</i><span class="flow-bubble flow-result">something useful ↗</span></div><span class="play-caption">A system, on the back of a napkin <span>+</span></span></summary><p>Good agent workflows start with clear intent. Give each part a purpose, connect the right context, and keep a human in the loop.</p></details><details class="play-card card-code" data-category="code"><summary><span class="torn lavender hand play-label">one more idea…</span><img src={`${base}/images/scrapbook/code.jpg`} alt="Code open on a laptop" width="600" height="700" loading="lazy"/><span class="play-caption">Curiosity is a pretty good starting point <span>+</span></span></summary><p>A notebook for the ideas that don't need a polished case study yet. Programming inspiration photo from Unsplash.</p></details></section>
  <p class="play-footnote hand">Not everything needs to be a project. Sometimes you just make a thing. ✳</p>
</BaseLayout>
